he trace, the trip record and the ledger. The money is arithmetic under POL-RD-02 on the metered fare: (3.80 base + 0.75 × 3.6 optimal km + 0.28 × 7.0 optimal min) × the surge multiplier of 1.0 read from the fare breakdown + 0.80 platform fee + 6.00 Changi Airport surcharge (properly chargeable, the trip originated at the airport on a Wednesday, outside the Friday–Sunday 17:00–23:59 peak window) + 0.00 ERP + 0.00 waiting time fee − 0.00 promo discount = 15.26 recomputed fare. Refund = charged_total 25.03 − 15.26 = 9.77, rounded up to 9.80. That is a partial refund: it is above the 0.50 de minimis and is 39.0% of the charge, well under the 90% that would make it a full refund. No ERP or toll pass-through correction is added on top of the 9.80 (POL-RD-09(1); the trip crossed no gantry and both ERP totals are 0.00), and the airport surcharge is not a billing-integrity defect. No trigger in POL-RD-10 fires: identifiers and timestamps agree across the three core artifacts, every charge line has a counterpart, and the refund is far below both 150.00 and the charged total. The ruling rests entirely on artifacts at weight 1.0, so the confidence cap is 1.0."
